Introduction to Python Data Types
Definition: A "type" is how Python represents different types of data.

Identifying Types: You can see the actual data type of an expression in Python by using the type command.

Primary Data Types
1. Integers (int)

Represent whole numbers (e.g., 11).

Can be positive or negative.

They have a finite range, though it is quite large.

2. Floats (float)

Represent real numbers (e.g., 21.213).

They include integers as well as the numbers in between them (e.g., numbers between 0 and 1, or between 0.5 and 0.6).

You can continue "zooming in" for different numbers between floats up to a limit, which is quite small.

3. Strings

Represent words.

Defined as a sequence of characters.

4. Booleans (bool)

Can only take on two values: True (must use an uppercase T) and False (must use an uppercase F).

Using the type command on a Boolean value returns the term bool.

Typecasting
Definition: Typecasting is the process of changing the type of an expression in Python.

Rules & Examples for Typecasting:

Int to Float: You can cast an integer (like 2) to a float (2.0). Nothing really changes.

Float to Int: You must be careful. Casting a float (like 1.1) to an integer (1) will cause you to lose some information.

String to Int: You can convert a string to an integer only if the string contains an integer value. If you try to convert a string that contains a non-integer value, Python will return an error.

Int or Float to String: You can convert both integers and floats to strings.

Boolean to Int or Float: Casting True gives you a 1. Casting False gives you a 0.

Int to Boolean: Casting a 1 gives you True. Casting a 0 gives you False.